Here is `POST /v1/bookings` written the quick way, as a route in `src/modules/bookings/bookings.routes.ts` that does everything itself:

```ts sketch
const bookings: Booking[] = [];
let nextId = 1001;

router.post("/", (req, res) => {
  const { roomId, startsAt, endsAt, attendees, note } = CreateBookingBody.parse(req.body);
  const room = rooms.find((r) => r.id === roomId);
  if (!room) return res.status(422).json({ error: "no room has this id" });
  const minutes = (endsAt.getTime() - startsAt.getTime()) / 60_000;
  if (minutes < 30 || minutes > 180) {
    return res.status(422).json({ error: "a booking lasts 30 minutes to 3 hours" });
  }
  if (startsAt <= new Date()) return res.status(422).json({ error: "must start in the future" });
  if (attendees > room.capacity) {
    return res.status(422).json({ error: `${room.name} holds at most ${room.capacity} people` });
  }
  const clash = bookings.some(
    (b) => b.status === "confirmed" && b.roomId === roomId && b.startsAt < endsAt && startsAt < b.endsAt,
  );
  if (clash) return res.status(409).json({ error: "room already booked" });
  const booking: Booking = {
    id: nextId++, roomId, userId: 1, startsAt, endsAt, attendees, note: note ?? null,
    status: "confirmed", version: 1, createdAt: new Date(),
  };
  bookings.push(booking);
  res.status(201).location(`/v1/bookings/${booking.id}`).json(booking);
});
```

It works, and for a weekend project it's fine. But look at how many kinds of work are mixed together in one function: reading HTTP, looking up the room, the booking rules, reading the clock, storing the booking, and shaping the JSON. It even sends the stored object straight back as JSON, so the times come out with milliseconds, and any field added to `Booking` later, an internal one included, goes to every client. Each kind of work changes for a different reason, and in one function, each change risks the others:

- **The database arrives in a few lessons.** Every line that touches `bookings`, the in-memory list, has to change, and those lines are scattered among the rules.
- **A rule changes.** The library extends bookings to 21 days ahead. You have to find the rule inside HTTP code, and the only way to test it is to send real requests.
- **A second way in.** A nightly job wants to cancel the bookings for a room that's closing, with the same rules. It isn't an HTTP request, so it can't call a handler.
- **Tests.** Checking that a three-and-a-half-hour booking is refused means starting a server, sending JSON, and reading JSON back, for what is really one comparison. And with the real clock inside the handler, a test that passes today fails next week, when its date is in the past.

## Separation of concerns

The fix is an old idea called **separation of concerns**: give each kind of work its own place, so each piece has one reason to change. A **concern** is one kind of work, like HTTP, the business rules, or storage. When the concerns are apart:

- Changing how data is stored touches only the storage code.
- A rule can be tested by calling one function with plain values and a fixed clock.
- The nightly job calls the same rule code the handler does.
- A newcomer knows where to look: HTTP problems in one place, rule bugs in another, slow queries in a third.

The cost is more files, and a little code to connect them. For an API that will live for years and be changed by many people, that cost pays for itself the first week. The rest of this lesson splits Roombook along exactly these lines, and every later lesson builds on the split.

Splitting a program by concern has a classic shape, older than the web, called **MVC**: Model, View, Controller. It was designed in the 1970s for desktop programs with windows and buttons. The **model** held the data and its rules, the **view** drew it on the screen, and the **controller** took the user's clicks and keys and told the model what to do. Web frameworks borrowed it for sites that build HTML pages on the server: the controller handles the request, the model loads the data, and the view is a template that fills the data into a page.

An API has no screen and no templates, but the same three jobs are still there, redrawn for a request and a response:

```diagram layers-and-mvc-mvc
MVC for a JSON API: the controller speaks HTTP, the model holds the domain, and the view is the JSON shape.
```

In step 1, a request arrives at the controller. In step 2, the controller calls the model to do the work. In step 3, it hands the result to the view, and in step 4, the view's JSON goes back as the response.

- The **model** is your domain: types like `Booking` and `Room`, and the rules about them. The **domain** is the real-world subject the software is about, here rooms, bookings, and the people who book them. The model knows nothing about HTTP or JSON.
- The **view** is the JSON you send back. It's a deliberate shape, not a dump of the model: camelCase field names, times as RFC 3339 text in UTC, and only what the client should see. In code, it's a small type or function that turns a model into that shape.
- The **controller** is the handler. It speaks HTTP: it reads the path, query, and body, parses them with the schemas from the validation lesson, calls the model, and turns the result into a status code, headers, and a view.

## Thin controllers

The rule that keeps MVC useful is that **controllers stay thin**. A controller translates between HTTP and the domain, and does nothing else. It never decides whether a booking is allowed, and never touches storage. A good test: if you can describe a line of a controller without the words request, response, status, header, or JSON, that line probably belongs somewhere else.

Here are Roombook's three pieces for bookings. The model, in `src/modules/bookings/bookings.model.ts`, is plain types with real `Date` objects in them:

```ts
export type BookingStatus = "confirmed" | "cancelled";

export type Booking = {
  id: number;
  roomId: number;
  userId: number;
  startsAt: Date;
  endsAt: Date;
  attendees: number;
  note: string | null;
  status: BookingStatus;
  version: number;
  createdAt: Date;
};

export type NewBooking = Omit<Booking, "id" | "status" | "version" | "createdAt">;
```

The view, in `src/modules/bookings/bookings.view.ts`, is the one from the request data lesson. It picks the fields, and `toUtcString` writes each time without the milliseconds `toISOString()` adds:

```ts
import { toUtcString } from "../../http/time.ts";
import type { Booking } from "./bookings.model.ts";

export function toBookingView(b: Booking) {
  return {
    id: b.id,
    roomId: b.roomId,
    userId: b.userId,
    startsAt: toUtcString(b.startsAt),
    endsAt: toUtcString(b.endsAt),
    attendees: b.attendees,
    note: b.note,
    status: b.status,
    version: b.version,
    createdAt: toUtcString(b.createdAt),
  };
}
```

And the controller, in `src/modules/bookings/bookings.controller.ts`, is short, and every line in it is about HTTP:

```ts
import type { Request, Response } from "express";
import { NotFoundError } from "../../domain/errors.ts";
import { CreateBookingBody } from "./bookings.schemas.ts";
import type { BookingService } from "./bookings.service.ts";
import { toBookingView } from "./bookings.view.ts";

const ADA = 1;

export function createBookingController(service: BookingService) {
  return {
    async create(req: Request, res: Response) {
      const input = CreateBookingBody.parse(req.body);
      const booking = await service.create({ ...input, note: input.note ?? null, userId: ADA });
      res.status(201).location(`/v1/bookings/${booking.id}`).json(toBookingView(booking));
    },

    async get(req: Request<{ bookingId: string }>, res: Response) {
      const id = Number(req.params.bookingId);
      if (!Number.isSafeInteger(id) || id < 1) throw new NotFoundError(`No booking with id ${req.params.bookingId}.`);
      res.json(toBookingView(await service.get(id)));
    },
  };
}

export type BookingController = ReturnType<typeof createBookingController>;
```

`createBookingController` takes the service and returns the two handlers, which reach the service through the closure, so they work when the router calls them on their own, as `router.post("/", controller.create)` does. `ADA` stands in for the signed-in user until the authentication lesson adds login. Each handler parses, builds the domain's `NewBooking`, calls the service, and sends a view. Neither catches anything: both are `async`, and Express 5 sends a rejected promise to the error handler, as the middleware lesson showed, so a `ZodError` or a `NotFoundError` becomes a problem with no `try`. The service is the part of the model that holds the rules, and the next part gives it its own layer.

## Where MVC stops

MVC says where HTTP ends and the domain begins, but its model is a big bucket: the rules, the data access, and the data types all land in it. For an API, that's too much in one place, so the next part splits the model in two.

The model splits along the line part 1 drew: rules on one side, storage on the other.

- A **service** holds the business rules for one area, like bookings. It's the code that answers "may Ada book Turing on Monday from 14:00 to 15:30?", and makes the booking if so. From the database lessons on, it has a second job: deciding which changes must happen together, in one database **transaction**, so they all happen or none do.
- A **repository** holds the data access for one kind of thing. It's the only code that knows where bookings live: today a list in memory, and from the raw SQL lesson on, a PostgreSQL table. That makes it the only code with SQL in it. Its methods are named in the domain's words, like `insert`, `findById`, and `countUpcoming`, not in the storage's.

With the controller from the last part, that makes three layers:

```diagram layers-and-mvc-layers
The layers call downward only. The model's types are shared by all three.
```

## Dependencies point one way

The arrows in the diagram only point down. The controller calls the service, the service calls the repository, and never the other way around. A service never reads a request or picks a status code, and a repository never applies a business rule. A layer **depends on** another when its code uses it, and this one-way rule about dependencies is what lets you replace a layer. As long as the repository keeps its methods, the service can't tell a list in memory from a database.

To make that real in code, the service doesn't depend on one particular repository. It depends on an **interface**, a description of the methods it needs, and any repository with those methods will do.

Here are the interface and today's implementation, in `src/modules/bookings/bookings.repository.ts`:

```ts
import { ConflictError } from "../../domain/errors.ts";
import type { Booking, NewBooking } from "./bookings.model.ts";

export type BookingRepository = {
  findById(id: number): Promise<Booking | undefined>;
  insert(booking: NewBooking): Promise<Booking>;
  countUpcoming(userId: number, now: Date): Promise<number>;
};

export function createInMemoryBookingRepository(clock: () => Date): BookingRepository {
  const rows = new Map<number, Booking>();
  let nextId = 1001;

  return {
    async findById(id) {
      const row = rows.get(id);
      return row && { ...row };
    },

    async insert(booking) {
      const clash = [...rows.values()].some((b) =>
        b.status === "confirmed" && b.roomId === booking.roomId &&
        b.startsAt < booking.endsAt && booking.startsAt < b.endsAt);
      if (clash) throw new ConflictError("booking-overlap", "The room is already booked then.");
      const row: Booking = { ...booking, id: nextId++, status: "confirmed", version: 1, createdAt: clock() };
      rows.set(row.id, row);
      return { ...row };
    },

    async countUpcoming(userId, now) {
      return [...rows.values()]
        .filter((b) => b.userId === userId && b.status === "confirmed" && b.startsAt > now).length;
    },
  };
}
```

`createInMemoryBookingRepository` is a **factory function**: it keeps the rows in variables only its methods can reach, and returns an object with the methods the type promises. The raw SQL lesson's `createBookingRepository(db)` returns the same type, with SQL inside. The interface shows only what this lesson's `create` and `get` need. The `update` and `cancel` that the earlier lessons' handlers called are still there, left out here, and the raw SQL lesson writes them in SQL, along with `listForUser`, for listing a member's bookings. The in-memory version hands out copies, `{ ...row }`, so code outside can't change a stored booking by changing the object it got back, which is how a database behaves too. It stamps `createdAt` from the clock it was given, the way a database fills in `now()`, and its `insert` refuses overlaps, standing in for the database constraint that the lesson on constraints and transactions builds. The service is a factory too, and takes everything it needs as one argument, typed by the interfaces, in `src/modules/bookings/bookings.service.ts`:

```ts sketch
export type BookingServiceDeps = {
  bookings: BookingRepository;
  rooms: RoomRepository;
  clock: () => Date;
};

export type BookingService = ReturnType<typeof createBookingService>;

export function createBookingService(deps: BookingServiceDeps) {
  const { bookings, rooms, clock } = deps;

  return {
    async create(input: NewBooking) { ... },
    async get(id: number) { ... },
  };
}
```

`clock` is in the list too. Reading the time is a dependency like any other, so a test can pass a fixed one, and Roombook passes the course's fixed clock. `ReturnType<typeof createBookingService>` names the service's type from the factory itself, so the two can't drift apart.

## DTOs at the edges

The types that cross the HTTP boundary are called **DTOs**, short for data transfer objects: the parsed request body coming in, from the validation lesson, and the view going out. They're kept apart from the model on purpose. The create DTO has no `id`, `status`, or `createdAt`, because the client mustn't set them. The view has no internal fields, because the client mustn't see them. And the model can gain a field, like a flag for a booking that staff created, without it leaking into a single response or becoming something a client can send.

Each layer needs the one below it. The controller needs a service, and the service needs two repositories and a clock. Something has to create them all, and hand each one its parts. The tempting shortcut is for each file to make its own, or import a shared global: a repository created at the top of the service's file. That hard-wires the choice. A test can't hand the service a fake repository, and swapping the store means editing the service.

The alternative is **dependency injection**. A piece of code doesn't create what it depends on, it receives it: through its constructor, or for a factory function, its arguments. The service says it needs a booking repository, a room repository, and a clock, and whoever creates it decides which ones. All the creating then happens in one place, at startup, called the **composition root**: the one spot that knows which implementations the running app uses.

Express has no built-in way to do this, and Roombook doesn't need one. Plain functions are enough. The composition root is `src/server.ts`, which builds every piece once, then the app:

```ts
import { createApp } from "./app.ts";
import { createInMemoryBookingRepository } from "./modules/bookings/bookings.repository.ts";
import { createBookingService } from "./modules/bookings/bookings.service.ts";
import { createInMemoryRoomRepository } from "./modules/rooms/rooms.repository.ts";

const fixed = process.env.HU_FIXED_TIME;
const clock = fixed ? () => new Date(fixed) : () => new Date();

const rooms = createInMemoryRoomRepository([
  { id: 1, name: "Turing", building: "main-library", capacity: 6, amenities: ["whiteboard", "screen"] },
  { id: 2, name: "Lovelace", building: "main-library", capacity: 4, amenities: ["whiteboard"] },
  { id: 3, name: "Hopper", building: "science-library", capacity: 8, amenities: ["screen", "video-call"] },
]);
const bookings = createInMemoryBookingRepository(clock);
const bookingService = createBookingService({ bookings, rooms, clock });

const app = createApp({
  bookingService,
  rooms,
  webOrigin: process.env.WEB_ORIGIN ?? "http://localhost:5173",
});

const port = Number(process.env.PORT ?? 4000);
const host = process.env.HOST ?? "127.0.0.1";
app.listen(port, host, (err) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  console.log(`Roombook API listening on http://${host}:${port}`);
});
```

`createApp`, in `src/app.ts`, takes what it needs as one argument, `deps`, and wires the routes to it:

```ts sketch
export type AppDeps = {
  bookingService: BookingService;
  rooms: RoomRepository;
  webOrigin: string;
};

export function createApp(deps: AppDeps) {
  const app = express();
  app.disable("x-powered-by");
  app.use(requestId);
  app.use(accessLog);
  app.use(helmet());
  app.use(cors({ origin: deps.webOrigin }));
  app.use(express.json({ limit: "100kb", type: ["application/json", "application/merge-patch+json"] }));

  app.use("/v1/rooms", roomRoutes(deps.rooms));
  app.use("/v1/bookings", bookingRoutes(createBookingController(deps.bookingService)));
  ...
}
```

The routers became functions too, `bookingRoutes(controller)`, so each gets its controller instead of importing one. Every piece is a factory function, a plain function that takes its dependencies and returns the piece, which is the most common way to wire an Express app. When the raw SQL lesson adds PostgreSQL, the only change is in `server.ts`: `createBookingRepository(pool)` where `createInMemoryBookingRepository(clock)` is now. And because `createApp` makes nothing itself, the testing lesson's tests call it with fakes, and send it requests without opening a port.

## Feature folders

The files are grouped by feature first, then by layer, so everything about bookings sits together:

```text
src/
  server.ts
  app.ts
  domain/
    errors.ts
  http/
    middleware/
    problems.ts
  modules/
    bookings/
      bookings.routes.ts
      bookings.controller.ts
      bookings.schemas.ts
      bookings.view.ts
      bookings.service.ts
      bookings.repository.ts
      bookings.model.ts
    rooms/
```

Grouping by layer instead, with one `controllers/` folder and one `services/` folder for the whole app, spreads one change across the whole tree. By feature, adding a field to bookings touches one folder.

The repository still keeps bookings in memory. The next lesson explains what a database does instead, and designs Roombook's tables.
